Look carefully at those numbers, for they are not random. As the sacrifice grows larger, everything that accompanies it grows larger too. The flour rises from one-tenth to two-tenths to three-tenths of an ephah. The oil rises from a fourth to a third to a half of a hin. The wine rises in exactly the same way. Nothing stays the same. When the offering is greater, the flour, the oil, and the wine must all increase together with it.

Here is the core principle, and we must state it plainly, because Yahweh Himself set the measure. The offering must always match the value of the sacrifice being presented. This was not a suggestion left to each worshipper's feelings. It was a fixed law. A man could not bring a great bull to the altar and then present beside it only a lamb-sized handful of flour. The accompanying gift had to rise to meet the greatness of the sacrifice. A large sacrifice required a large offering. And this simple law of the altar carries a searching lesson for every New Creation, for we cannot bring a bull-sized understanding of Christ and then offer God only a lamb-sized portion of ourselves.

The governing word for this whole study is spoken by our Master Himself. He said, "When someone has been given much, much will be required in return; and when someone has been entrusted with much, even more will be required" (Luke 12:48). This is the law of the flour and the oil and the wine, spoken in the words of Christ. The measure of what Yahweh requires of us rises with the measure of what He has given us. The more we have received, the more is asked of us in return. This is not unfair. It is the very justice of God. To the one who has been shown little, little is required. But to the one who has been shown much, much is required.

So let us turn the three animals into a mirror. There is a lamb-level understanding of Christ, a ram-level understanding, and a bull-level understanding. These are not three different Saviours, and they are not three different sacrifices. There is only one sacrifice of Christ. But some see that one sacrifice as a young child sees it, with simple and precious first faith. Others have grown, and they see it as those who have consecrated their whole lives. And others have grown further still, and they behold the depth of that one sacrifice with the clear eyes of the mature. It is the same cross, but seen with increasing clarity. And here is the point we must not miss: as our sight of the sacrifice grows, the measure of our own offering must grow to match it.

Consider what the flour, the oil, and the wine each represent, for they are three different kinds of response, and all three had to rise together. The fine flour, sifted and pure, speaks of character; the daily life ground fine and made pure before God. The oil speaks of the Holy Spirit and the Spirit-filled life poured through us. And the wine, poured out entirely before Yahweh, speaks of devotion; a life gladly poured out for Him. Notice that when the sacrifice increased, not one of these was allowed to remain small. The flour rose, the oil rose, and the wine rose, all together. It was not enough for a worshipper to increase his flour while keeping his oil and his wine small. In the same way, it is not enough for us to grow in only one area. Our character, our walk in the Spirit, and our poured-out devotion must all rise together as our knowledge of Christ increases.

This brings us to a searching question, and it is the very heart of today's study. Does our giving match our knowledge? This is the question the measurements press upon each of us. It is easy to grow in one thing and neglect the others. It is possible to grow greatly in understanding while our devotion remains exactly where it was years ago. And when that happens, we are out of proportion. We are bringing the bull to the altar and setting beside it only the lamb's small measure of flour. The greatness of what we know is no longer matched by the greatness of what we give.

Let us make this very personal, for it is easy to hide behind general words. Picture a believer who has studied the Scriptures deeply. He understands the Greek text. He can trace the types and shadows through the Tabernacle.

He grasps doctrines that many have never even considered. His knowledge is bull-sized. But when we look at his devotion, his obedience, his patience, his humility, his surrender to the will of God, we find that these have hardly grown at all. His understanding is that of a mature man, but his surrender is still that of a spiritual infant. His flour has never risen to match his sacrifice. This is not a small thing. A great knowledge of Christ joined to a small offering of self is a life that is out of proportion before God. The deeper we see, the more is required of the way we live.

This is why the Scripture reminds us of our accountability. Paul writes, "Now, a person who is put in charge as a manager must be faithful" (1 Corinthians 4:2). Knowledge is never neutral. It does not simply make us wiser; it makes us responsible. Every truth Yahweh reveals to us becomes a trust that we must steward faithfully. To be given a great understanding of Christ and then to live carelessly is to be an unfaithful steward of what we have received. The one who knows much is not merely blessed; he is accountable. The rising measure of the offering is really the rising measure of our responsibility before God.

But we must be very careful here, so that no young or new believer is crushed by these words. Let us remember the widow and her two small coins. Our Master watched the rich putting large gifts into the treasury, and then He saw a poor widow put in two tiny coins, and He said that she had put in more than all the others, "for they gave a tiny part of their surplus, but she, poor as she is, has given everything she had" (Mark 12:44). This does not contradict the lesson of the offerings. It completes it. The law of the offerings was always about proportion; the gift rising in proportion to the sacrifice. And the widow's mite is proportion too. She gave a lamb-sized gift in the eyes of men, but it was bull-sized in proportion to all that she had. So if you are young in the faith, or new to the truth, do not measure yourself against those who have walked for many years. Yahweh does not ask you to bring the bull's portion while you still have only the lamb's understanding. He asks only that your flour, your oil, and your wine be full according to what you have received. The widow who gives her all, though it is small, brings a whole offering. It is not the size of the gift that God weighs, but whether it truly matches what we have been given.

So let us turn the measuring cup outward as well as inward, and let us not end in guilt but in glad invitation. If our sight of Christ has grown, let our flour, our oil, and our wine grow with it. Growth in devotion is not a heavy burden laid upon us from outside. It is the natural fruit of growth in understanding. The more clearly we see the greatness of the sacrifice Christ made, the more gladly and the more fully we will pour ourselves out in return. As this year unfolds and Yahweh opens more of His Word to us, let our offering rise to meet it. Let us not be those who know much and give little, but those whose devotion keeps pace with their knowledge, so that the measure of our lives may always match the measure of what our God has so graciously given us.
